/-!
# Testing h descent for stacks in groupoids on the two generator almost blowup squares

Stacks 0EX9 characterizes the stacks in groupoids on the big h site `(Sch/S)_h` of Stacks 0EU1
by three conditions on a category `p : 𝒮 ⥤ (Sch/S)_h` fibred in groupoids:

1. `𝒮` is a stack in groupoids for the Zariski topology;
2. given a morphism `f : X ⟶ Y` of `(Sch/S)_h` with `Y` affine and `f` surjective, flat, proper
   and of finite presentation, the functor
   `𝒮_Y ⟶ 𝒮_X ×_{𝒮_{X ×_Y X}} 𝒮_X` is an equivalence of categories;
3. for an almost blow up square as in Example 0EVG or 0EVH in the category `(Sch/S)_h`, the
   functor `𝒮_X ⟶ 𝒮_Z ×_{𝒮_E} 𝒮_{X'}` is an equivalence of categories.

It is the stack analogue of Stacks 0EVI, which is the same criterion for a presheaf of sets, and
which is carried in this development by
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Topologies.HTopology.GeneratorAlmostBlowUpSheafCriterion`.  The two
shapes of square are the ones defined there: `AlgebraicGeometry.IsOneGeneratorAlmostBlowUpModel`
for Example 0EVG and `AlgebraicGeometry.IsTwoGeneratorAlmostBlowUpModel` for Example 0EVH, and they
are reused verbatim, so conditions (2) and (3) here range over exactly the squares conditions (2),
(3) and (4) there range over.

## Main definitions

* `AlgebraicGeometry.BigHOver.zariskiPrecoverage` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.BigHOver.zariskiTopology`: the Zariski topology on `(Sch/S)_h`, generated by
  the families of morphisms of the site whose underlying family of morphisms of schemes is a
  Zariski covering.  This is the topology condition (1) refers to; it is *not* a second h
  topology, and the h topology itself remains `AlgebraicGeometry.BigHOver.topology`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.BigHOver.twoFibreProductComparison`: for a cartesian square of the site, the
  canonical functor `𝒮_X ⟶ 𝒮_Z ×_{𝒮_E} 𝒮_{X'}` from the fibre category over the base into the
  two-fibre product of the fibre categories over the centre and the modification.  Both displayed
  functors of the source are instances of it: condition (3) is the square `(z, b)` with
  exceptional corner `E`, and condition (2) is the square of `f` with itself, whose exceptional
  corner is `X ×_Y X`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.BigHOver.IsZariskiStackInGroupoids`,
  `AlgebraicGeometry.BigHOver.IsAffineFlatProperFinitePresentationStack`,
  `AlgebraicGeometry.BigHOver.IsOneGeneratorAlmostBlowUpStack` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.BigHOver.IsTwoGeneratorAlmostBlowUpStack`: conditions (1), (2) and the two
  halves of condition (3).

## Main results

* `CategoryTheory.Functor.IsStackInGroupoids.mono`: a stack in groupoids for a topology is a
  stack in groupoids for every coarser topology.  The conditions of Stacks 02ZI are indexed by
  the covering families of the topology, so this is immediate from the definition.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.BigHOver.zariskiTopology_le_topology`: the Zariski topology on `(Sch/S)_h` is
  coarser than the h topology.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.BigHOver.isZariskiStackInGroupoids_of_isStackInGroupoids`: **Stacks 0EX9,
  necessity of condition (1).**  A stack in groupoids for the h topology on `(Sch/S)_h` is a stack
  in groupoids for the Zariski topology.

## Implementation notes

**Which notion of stack.**  `CategoryTheory.Functor.IsStackInGroupoids` (Stacks 02ZI) is used, not
`CategoryTheory.Functor.IsStack` (Stacks 026F): the source says *stack in groupoids*, and 02ZI is
the definition it refers to.  The two are distinct predicates in this library — 02ZI is phrased
with covering families whose overlaps are supplied as genuine fibre products in the base, 026F
with covering sieves and the chosen pullbacks of a fibred category — and
`CategoryTheory.Functor.IsStack.isStackInGroupoids` relates them in one direction only.

**The Zariski topology on the site.**  The h topology on `(Sch/S)_h` is
`AlgebraicGeometry.BigHOver.topology`, the localization at `S` of the topology generated by the
site's own covering families.  There was no Zariski topology on this category, so one is built
here in the same shape: `AlgebraicGeometry.BigHOver.zariskiPrecoverage` collects the presieves
that are the presieve of a family of the site whose image under
`AlgebraicGeometry.BigHOver.toScheme` is a Zariski covering of schemes, exactly the families the
sheaf-level condition (1), `AlgebraicGeometry.BigHOver.IsZariskiSheaf`, is quantified over, and
`AlgebraicGeometry.BigHOver.zariskiTopology` is the Grothendieck topology it generates.  Nothing
about the h topology is redefined.

**The two-fibre product and the comparison functor.**  `𝒮_X` is the fibre category
`CategoryTheory.Functor.Fiber`, the pullback functors are
`CategoryTheory.Functor.IsFibered.fiberPullback`, and `𝒮_Z ×_{𝒮_E} 𝒮_{X'}` is Mathlib's
`CategoryTheory.Limits.CategoricalPullback`, whose objects are triples consisting of an object of
`𝒮_Z`, an object of `𝒮_{X'}` and an isomorphism between their images in `𝒮_E`.  That is the
two-fibre product of categories the source's displayed functors land in, taken literally; in
particular no cocycle condition over a triple overlap is imposed, so neither condition is the
condition that a category of descent data be equivalent to the fibre over the base.  The
structural isomorphism of the comparison functor is
`CategoryTheory.Functor.IsFibered.CartStruct.cmpIso` applied to the two cartesian structures on
the two composites around the square, which agree because the square commutes.

**What is proved.**  Only the necessity of condition (1) is proved.  Necessity of conditions (2)
and (3) is omitted in the source itself, and sufficiency is there deduced from Stacks 0EVI, whose
own sufficiency direction is not available in this development: the module documentation of
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Topologies.HTopology.GeneratorAlmostBlowUpSheafCriterion` records
that what it still assumes is
`AlgebraicGeometry.GeneratorAlmostBlowUpSquaresSuffice`, the reduction of condition (3) of Stacks
0EVF to the two shapes, which is the content of the proof of Stacks 0EVI itself.  The sufficiency
direction of Stacks 0EVF is not what is missing: it is
`AlgebraicGeometry.affineAlmostBlowUpHSheafCriterion` there, proved from the affine-base case of
Stacks 0EVC, `AlgebraicGeometry.finitePresentationHasAlmostBlowUpFlattening`.  The three
conditions are nevertheless all defined here, so that the criterion this file is about is stated
in full and the remaining work is exactly the two unproved implications.

**Condition (2) as stated is false for h stacks.**  The literal condition fails for every category
fibred in groupoids `𝒮` over the site which is a stack for the Zariski topology and has an object
`x` over an affine object `Y` with an automorphism `g ≠ 1`; for instance the h stack of h torsors
under a nontrivial finite constant group, at the trivial torsor.  Take for `f` the fold map
`∇ : Y ⊔ Y ⟶ Y`, which is an isomorphism on each summand, hence surjective, flat, proper and of
finite presentation.  Write `Y₁, Y₂` for the two summands of `X = Y ⊔ Y`, so that `X ×_Y X` is the
disjoint union of four copies `Y_{ij}` of `Y`, with `pr₁` the identity `Y_{ij} ⟶ Y_i` and `pr₂` the
identity `Y_{ij} ⟶ Y_j`.  By the Zariski condition `𝒮_X ≃ 𝒮_Y × 𝒮_Y` and
`𝒮_{X ×_Y X} ≃ ∏_{i,j} 𝒮_Y`, so an object of `𝒮_X ×_{𝒮_{X ×_Y X}} 𝒮_X` is a pair `(a₁, a₂)`,
a pair `(b₁, b₂)` and four isomorphisms `φ_{ij} : a_i ≅ b_j`, and the comparison functor sends `y`
to `((y, y), (y, y), (1)_{ij})`.  Let `a = b = (x, x)`, `φ₁₁ = φ₁₂ = φ₂₁ = 1` and `φ₂₂ = g`.
An isomorphism from the image of some `y` to this object is a quadruple `u_i, v_j : y ≅ x` with
`φ_{ij} ∘ u_i = v_j` for all `i, j`: the equations for `(1,1)`, `(1,2)` and `(2,1)` force
`u₁ = v₁ = v₂ = u₂`, and then the one for `(2,2)` reads `g ∘ u₂ = u₂`, so `g = 1`.  Hence the
object is not in the essential image, and the comparison functor of the source's condition (2) is
not an equivalence.  The descent data of `{∇}` exclude that object: for them the two objects over
`X` coincide, and the condition over the diagonal `X ⟶ X ×_Y X` forces `φ₁₁ = φ₂₂ = 1`.  This is
why the criterion is
registered with condition (2) in its descent form,
`AlgebraicGeometry.BigHOver.IsAffineFlatProperFinitePresentationDescent`; the computation is not
formalized, the coproduct `Y ⊔ Y` of the site and the decomposition of the fibres over it being
absent from this development.

